WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.980
Most people will never see the Strait of

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:02.540
Hormuz.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.200
It is a narrow passage between Iran and

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:05.580
Oman.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.440
But when ships slow down here, the

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.180
consequences travel far beyond the Gulf.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.545
On August 2, President Donald Trump said

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.520
the United States would hold off on new

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.760
strikes after claiming the outlines of a

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.780
deal with Iran.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.920
He said the deal would include opening

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.580
Hormuz.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.690
Iran's foreign ministry said there was no

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.620
agreement to return the strait to its

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.450
prewar status.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.150
So the headline is not that the strait is

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:28.380
open.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.290
It is that control of this map is still

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.500
being negotiated.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.080
Start with the geography.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.980
Tankers leaving the Persian Gulf have to

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.940
pass through this gateway before reaching

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.320
the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.490
The traffic then fans out toward India,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.460
China, South Korea, and Japan.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.750
The route is short on the map, but there

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.740
are few practical substitutes.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.650
That is why a local incident becomes a

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.580
global story.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.570
AP reported drone attacks in Kuwait and a

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.370
tanker hit near Oman.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.120
And AP says Iraq is now trying to move

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.410
more oil north through a pipeline to

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.050
Turkey, targeting at least 750,000 barrels

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:05.620
a day.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.280
An alternative, but not a replacement for

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.340
Gulf shipping.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.140
The numbers explain the leverage.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.940
The Energy Information Administration

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.040
estimates about 20 million barrels of oil

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.400
per day crossed Hormuz in 2024, about a

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.240
fifth of global petroleum liquids

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.330
consumption.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.680
About a fifth of global liquefied natural

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.270
gas trade also passed through it, much of

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.500
it headed to Asia.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.060
So what happens next?

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.080
A deal could reopen the route, but the

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.810
positions are still opposed.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.420
Washington wants free passage without a

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.360
new toll regime.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.180
Tehran says the post-war arrangement

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.750
cannot simply go back to the old one.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.090
Until those conditions are settled, every

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.670
tanker, insurance quote, an alternate

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.260
pipeline is part of the negotiation.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.040
This is the pressure point, not a dot on a

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.380
map, but the switch between a regional war

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.120
and a worldwide energy problem.
